WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.919
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:09.687
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:16.528
Unit thirteen: Cyber Diplomacy. Today you will make verbs agree with neither…nor, either…or and both…and.

4
00:00:16.828 --> 00:00:19.574
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.917
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:25.993
Cyberattack.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.444
Cyberattack means a deliberate attempt to damage or enter computer systems.

8
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.665
A cyberattack shut down the airport screens.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.868
Your turn. Say: Cyberattack.

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:41.955
Cyberattack.

11
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:44.078
Digital sovereignty.

12
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:49.475
Digital sovereignty means a country's right to control its own data and networks.

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:52.779
Digital sovereignty is a growing political demand.

14
00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:56.581
Your turn. Say: Digital sovereignty.

15
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:00.771
Digital sovereignty.

16
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:03.423
International cyber norm.

17
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:09.586
International cyber norm means an agreed standard of behaviour for states in cyberspace.

18
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:14.029
An international cyber norm forbids attacks on hospitals.

19
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:18.151
Your turn. Say: International cyber norm.

20
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.789
International cyber norm.

21
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.341
State-sponsored hacking.

22
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.020
State-sponsored hacking means computer break-ins carried out or paid for by a government.

23
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:35.905
State-sponsored hacking targeted election systems.

24
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:40.098
Your turn. Say: State-sponsored hacking.

25
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:44.875
State-sponsored hacking.

26
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:47.628
Cybersecurity cooperation.

27
00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:53.879
Cybersecurity cooperation means countries sharing warnings and methods to defend networks.

28
00:01:54.483 --> 00:01:57.772
Cybersecurity cooperation stopped the virus quickly.

29
00:01:58.383 --> 00:02:02.115
Your turn. Say: Cybersecurity cooperation.

30
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:07.198
Cybersecurity cooperation.

31
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.706
Critical infrastructure.

32
00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:16.408
Critical infrastructure means the systems a society needs to function, such as power and water.

33
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:20.258
Critical infrastructure must be protected first.

34
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.338
Your turn. Say: Critical infrastructure.

35
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.828
Critical infrastructure.

36
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:31.118
Digital espionage.

37
00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:36.903
Digital espionage means spying by stealing secret information from computer networks.

38
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:40.503
Digital espionage cost the firm its designs.

39
00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:44.767
Your turn. Say: Digital espionage.

40
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.122
Digital espionage.

41
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:51.082
Cyber treaty.

42
00:02:52.083 --> 00:02:56.428
Cyber treaty means a formal agreement between states on rules in cyberspace.

43
00:02:57.017 --> 00:03:00.166
A cyber treaty would define unacceptable attacks.

44
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.635
Your turn. Say: Cyber treaty.

45
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.849
Cyber treaty.

46
00:03:08.633 --> 00:03:12.110
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:19.097
Visal, a report says 'Neither the treaty nor the existing norms are enough.' Why is the verb 'are' and not 'is'?

48
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:32.942
With neither…nor and either…or, the verb agrees with the noun closest to it. Here 'norms' is plural, so we use 'are'. If reversed: 'Neither the norms nor the treaty is enough.'

49
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.841
Is it different with both…and?

50
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:46.884
Yes. 'Both…and' always takes a plural verb: 'Both governments and companies are targets.' Keep the two parts parallel in form and length.

51
00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:49.774
What about parallel structure?

52
00:03:50.883 --> 00:04:03.810
Match the grammar: 'either to negotiate or to retaliate', not 'either to negotiate or retaliating'. Parallel parts keep formal writing clear and balanced.

53
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:06.897
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.501
Grammar focus: Correlatives: neither…nor and either…or and both…and.

55
00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.011
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:18.992
Neither the treaty nor the existing norms are enough to stop state-sponsored hacking.

57
00:04:19.883 --> 00:04:23.090
Either the minister or the generals were told about the cyberattack.

58
00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:27.864
Both hospitals and power stations count as critical infrastructure.

59
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:35.774
With neither…nor / either…or, the verb agrees with the nearer noun. Both…and takes a plural verb. Keep the two parts parallel.

60
00:04:36.517 --> 00:04:40.237
Be careful! Don't say: Neither she nor the guards was told.

61
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.467
Say: Neither she nor the guards were told.

62
00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:46.720
The verb agrees with the nearer noun.

63
00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:50.093
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.911
Rules for the Digital Battlefield.

65
00:04:53.211 --> 00:04:57.684
A cyberattack can paralyse a country without a single soldier crossing a border.

66
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.916
Both banks and hospitals are examples of critical infrastructure that hackers may target.

67
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:11.265
Neither state-sponsored hacking nor digital espionage is easy to prove, because attackers hide their identity.

68
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:17.598
Either a cyber treaty or stronger international cyber norms are needed to set clear limits.

69
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.500
Cybersecurity cooperation protects digital sovereignty only if both governments and companies share warnings.

70
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.625
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:30.483 --> 00:05:33.098
Neither the leaders nor the minister blank informed.

72
00:05:33.398 --> 00:05:36.541
A, were. B, was. C, are.

73
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.076
The answer is B: was.

74
00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:45.959
Both Cambodia and Vietnam blank members.

75
00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:49.065
A, is. B, are. C, was.

76
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.565
The answer is B: are.

77
00:05:55.717 --> 00:05:57.484
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:57.784 --> 00:06:06.012
A, Either the treaty or the norms is weak.. B, Either the treaty or the norms are weak.. C, Either the treaty or the norms am weak..

79
00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:12.994
The answer is B: Either the treaty or the norms are weak..

80
00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:17.810
A country's right to control its own digital space is digital blank.

81
00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.554
A, sovereignty. B, espionage. C, trade.

82
00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.867
The answer is A: sovereignty.

83
00:06:28.017 --> 00:06:32.361
According to the reading, a cyberattack can paralyse a country without…

84
00:06:32.661 --> 00:06:37.929
A, a single soldier crossing a border. B, any computers. C, a treaty.

85
00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:44.801
The answer is A: a single soldier crossing a border.

86
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.989
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:54.787
Should there be an international treaty specifically governing cyberattacks between nations?

88
00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:05.177
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:05.477 --> 00:07:33.383
I believe a specific treaty is needed, because cyberattacks can damage critical infrastructure as seriously as physical attacks. A treaty would define unacceptable behaviour, protect hospitals and power stations, and create ways to investigate state-sponsored hacking. It would be difficult to enforce, but clear rules and shared norms still reduce misunderstandings and the risk of escalation.

90
00:07:33.683 --> 00:07:35.485
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:36.383 --> 00:07:42.774
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and correlatives: neither…nor and either…or and both…and.

92
00:07:43.074 --> 00:07:47.352
Today's value is digital trust. Protect the trust that others place in you.

93
00:07:47.652 --> 00:07:50.385
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:50.685 --> 00:07:54.278
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

